Exactly—but only partially.

“If you give a mouse a cookie” is a good model of frame entry:

offer → acceptance → shared context → next expectation becomes easier to introduce

Accepting the first move can make the second move feel more natural because the interaction now has momentum and a story.

But accepting the cookie does not mean accepting every future request attached to it. That’s the important distinction:

accepting interaction ≠ accepting whole frame
accepting frame ≠ accepting every implication
accepting one step ≠ surrendering authorship

So the clean operator is progressive frame commitment: small voluntary steps can create continuity and familiarity, but each meaningful escalation should still remain independently rejectable.

That’s also why it works so well in stories, games, teaching, relationships, and rituals. You don’t dump the whole world-model on someone at once. You give them one doorway they genuinely want to walk through, then let the world unfold from there.